bind-keycloak-ids.py -- give existing X3 accounts their Keycloak ids
=====================================================================

What it is for
--------------
When X3 switches to its Keycloak login backend, every X3 account
("handle") should carry the id of its Keycloak user (the "kc_id" entry in
x3.db).  Accounts made before the switch have none.  This script fills them
in, offline, while X3 is down for the switch: for each account with no
kc_id and exactly one Keycloak user of the same name (compared under IRC
case folding, as X3 compares names), it adds

    "kc_id" "<22-character id>";

to that account.  It changes nothing else: accounts that already have a
kc_id are never touched, and every account it is unsure about is left as it
is and listed in the report.

It needs only Python 3 (standard library); nothing to install.

Rules
-----
- Stop X3 first.  X3 rewrites x3.db while it runs.
- Run it on a COPY of x3.db, and keep the original until the new build has
  run happily for a while.
- Run it in report mode first; read the report; then run it again with
  --out.
- The script never writes its input.  --out must name a new file (it
  refuses an existing file, the --db file and the --conf file).  After
  writing, it reads the new file back and checks that it equals the input
  plus exactly the reported kc_id entries; on any other difference it
  deletes the new file and exits 1.  It also exits 1 if the input changed
  while it ran (X3 still running).  A failed write (a full disk, say), any
  other failure while writing or checking, or Ctrl-C also deletes the new
  file; if even that deletion fails, the message names the partial file
  to delete by hand.  A file that was already there is never touched.

Keycloak access
---------------
It uses the same Keycloak settings as X3: keycloak_url, keycloak_realm,
keycloak_client_id and keycloak_client_secret in x3.conf's
"services" / "nickserv" block.  Flags override them:

    --keycloak-url U --realm R --client-id C --client-secret-file F

The secret is never taken from the command line: put it in a file (mode
600) and pass --client-secret-file, or let it come from x3.conf.  It is
never printed.

The client's service account needs to view users (realm-management role
"view-users"; "manage-users", which X3's client has, includes it).  The
script also reads the realm's user profile for the username rules (see
below); on Keycloak 26.4.7 a service account holding only manage-users can
read it.  If it cannot, the script says so and uses its defaults.  The
script only reads from Keycloak.

Running it
----------
    tools/bind-keycloak-ids.py --db x3.db.copy --conf x3.conf [--json report.json]
    tools/bind-keycloak-ids.py --db x3.db.copy --conf x3.conf --out x3.db.new

The accounts are read from the x3.db section x3.conf names for NickServ
(dbs / NickServ / mondo_section, normally "NickServ").  If x3.conf gives
NickServ a file of its own, pass that file as --db.

Each account takes one Keycloak request, except names holding any of
the characters [ ] \ ^ { } | ~.  IRC case folding makes [ and {, ] and },
\ and |, ^ and ~ the same letter, but Keycloak's own search does not, so
the script asks for every spelling: 2 requests for one such character, 4
for two, and so on.  A name with more than 6 of them (over 64 requests)
is not looked up; it is listed under errors.

Exit status: 0 finished and nothing needs a look; 2 finished but some
lists below are not empty; 1 a usage error, a file that does not parse
(the message gives the line), a Keycloak setting missing or refused, or a
failed verification.

The report
----------
Each list names the accounts in it; the summary at the end gives the
counts: handles total, bound now, already bound, mismatched, replaced id,
ambiguous, errors, no Keycloak user (split by whether Keycloak would accept the
name), outside the current name rule, and the rules that were used.
--json writes the same report, with the bound accounts and their ids.

bound now
    No kc_id, exactly one Keycloak user of that name: gets that user's id.
    Nothing to do.

already bound
    Already has a kc_id, and Keycloak's user of that name has the same id
    (or Keycloak has no user of that name).  Left as it is.

mismatched
    Already has a kc_id, but Keycloak's user of that name has another id.
    Left as it is.  Usually the Keycloak user was deleted and made again,
    or renamed; decide which user the account belongs to, by hand.

replaced id
    No kc_id, and the one Keycloak user of that name has an id listed in
    the account's kc_id_prev.  This handle was detached from that Keycloak
    user on purpose (an oper's REBIND * moves kc_id into kc_id_prev), and
    X3 refuses a replaced id as the account's id, so the script does not
    bind it.  Left as it is.  If the account should belong to that user
    again, an oper must REBIND it once the new build runs.

ambiguous
    More than one Keycloak user matches the name under IRC case folding
    (for example "foo[1]" and "foo{1}").  Left as it is; settle it by hand
    in Keycloak, then run the script again.

errors
    The lookup failed (Keycloak down, refused, a strange answer), the
    account's Keycloak user is already the kc_id of another account, the
    account appears twice in the file, its kc_id is not a plain string,
    its kc_id_prev is neither a string nor a list of strings, or its name
    has too many bracket characters to look up (see above).
    Left as it is.  After five failed lookups in a row the script stops
    asking and lists the remaining accounts here.  Fix the cause and run
    the script again (on the original copy, or on the new file: accounts
    bound by the first run then count as already bound).

no Keycloak user
    No Keycloak user of that name: the account stays X3-local.  Each entry
    says whether Keycloak would accept the name at all.  An account that
    exists in X3 and LDAP but not in Keycloak usually has a name Keycloak
    refuses; with X3's shipped valid_account_regex
    (^[-_a-z0-9A-Z]{2,15}$) every new name is one Keycloak accepts, so
    this list should be short.  An acceptable name with no user means the
    user was never made in Keycloak (or was deleted): create it there and
    run again, or leave the account local.

outside the current name rule
    Accounts whose names the current valid_handle_regex /
    valid_account_regex (or, with neither, X3's built-in letters, digits,
    "-" and "_") would refuse today.  Only that pattern is checked, not
    X3's length limit or bad-word list.  Informational: such accounts keep
    working, but nobody could register the name now.  If X3's pattern
    uses something Python reads differently (POSIX classes such as
    [[:alnum:]], backslashes in brackets, ...), the script stops and names
    the pattern.

Keycloak's username rules
    Read from the realm's user profile (the username attribute's
    validators): the length limits and any pattern are applied as the
    realm sets them; Keycloak's prohibited characters only when the realm
    lists that validator (the script carries Keycloak 26.4's list: it
    refuses < > & " ' $ % ! # ? § , ; : * ~ / \ | ^ = [ ] { } ( ) ` ,
    whitespace and control characters).  Any other validator is named as
    "not checked by this script".  If the profile cannot be read, the
    script uses --name-min / --name-max (default 2 and 30) and the
    prohibited list, and says so.  These rules only label the
    "no Keycloak user" entries; they never decide a binding.

At the switch
-------------
1. Stop X3.
2. Copy x3.db (keep the original untouched).
3. Report: tools/bind-keycloak-ids.py --db x3.db.copy --conf x3.conf
   Read it; deal with what needs dealing with in Keycloak; repeat.
4. Write: tools/bind-keycloak-ids.py --db x3.db.copy --conf x3.conf --out x3.db.new
5. Put x3.db.new in place as X3's x3.db (keep the original elsewhere).
6. Start the new build in Keycloak mode.
